Orthostatic Cognitive Dysfunction in
Postural Tachycardia Syndrome After
Rapid Water Drinking
Belén Rodriguez 1 , Raya Zimmermann 1 , Klemens Gutbrod 2 , Doerthe Heinemann 2 and
Werner J. Z’Graggen 1,2*
1
Department of Neurosurgery, Inselspital, Bern University Hospital, University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland, 2 Department
of Neurology, Inselspital, Bern University Hospital, University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland

Background: Postural tachycardia syndrome (POTS) is a form of autonomic
dysregulation and is characterized by an excessive heart rate (HR) increment upon
the upright body position while blood pressure is maintained. Patients experience
typical symptoms of orthostatic intolerance such as dizziness, nausea and cognitive
impairments. The present study assessed position-dependent attentional and cognitive
functioning in POTS patients compared to healthy subjects and tested the response of
cognitive performance to acute water intake.

